#We define and create a list to store and call items
#To create a list we use the square brackets [] - we can store any type of data in a list (numbers, strings, booleans, etc.) - we can also store other lists inside a list (nested lists)

students = ["Peter", "Paul", "Mary", "John"]
print(students) 

#If we have a variable (students) and we want to go inside that variable/list and access/use a specific item in that variable/list we can use the index 
print(students[0])  #This will print "Peter"
print(students[1])  #This will print "Paul"
print(students[2])  #This will print "Mary"
print(students[3])  #This will print "John"

#We can also use reverse indexing to access items in a list - the last item in the list is -1, the second last item is -2 etc.

print(students[-1]) #This will print "John"


#We can also use a for loop to iterate over the items in a list
#This will now print all items in our list/variable
#The variable student in the for loop is a temporary variable that takes on the value of each item in the list as the loop iterates over it (always receiving the next value in the variable/list students)
#We could name this variable anything we want as it is just a temporary variable/placeholder for the value of each item in the list as the loop interates over it
for student in students:
    print(student) 


#DYNMIC SOLUTION TO PRINT INDEX AND VALUE OF ITEMS IN A LIST
#We can now create a dynamic solution to print the index of each item in the list and the value of the item in the list
#There is a function called len() that will return the length of a list
#This is useful when we want to know how many items are in a list - we can use this in a for loop to iterate over the items in a list based on the length of the list

#Instead of typing out the index of each item in the list we can create a dynamic solution that will work for any length of a list
#We use the function len() to get the length of the list and use that in the range() function to create a sequence of numbers starting at 0 and ending at the last item/index of the list 
#We then use that sequence of numbers as the index to access each item in the list and print the index and the value of the item in the list
#This will work for any length of a list as it is dynamic and will always get the length of the list and create a sequence of numbers based on that length
for index in range(len(students)):
    print(index, students[index])
#The square brackets [] are used to access the item in the list based on the index - index changes during the loop/with each iteration and takes on the value of each item in the sequence created by the range() function



